= Learning — Technical =

== Operational definition ==
Learning is often inferred from a relatively persistent change in a performance function after experience:

<pre>
experience E
   ↓
internal / behavioral change
   ↓
later performance differs
</pre>

Observed performance also depends on motivation, fatigue, context, and retrieval, so performance change is not a pure learning measure.

== Associative learning ==
Classical and instrumental paradigms model changes in predictive or action–outcome relationships.

Modern accounts include prediction-error, Bayesian, attentional, and representational mechanisms.

== Statistical learning ==
Learners can acquire distributional regularities from sequences or structured environments, sometimes without explicit instruction.

Measures may capture familiarity, prediction, segmentation, or implicit sensitivity rather than one unitary mechanism.

== Transfer ==
Near transfer reuses highly similar structure; far transfer requires broader generalization.

Claims of far transfer require careful controls because test familiarity, motivation, and strategy can mimic genuine generalization.

